First determine whether the bottleneck is JavaScript work, native rendering, images, or excessive measurement; I would profile before changing knobs. Use `FlatList` rather than eagerly mounting a large `ScrollView`, give rows stable keys, and keep `renderItem` and row props stable so unrelated state does not redraw every cell. For fixed-size rows, `getItemLayout` lets the list skip measurement. Tune the initial and windowed rendering only against device measurements: rendering too much costs memory, while rendering too little can expose blank content during fast scrolling. Remember that `FlatList` is a `PureComponent`; values outside `data` that affect rendering must be passed through changed props such as `extraData`. Test on a representative lower-end Android device, not only a simulator.

```tsx caption="Stable keys, explicit dependencies, and known row geometry help a virtualized list."
<FlatList
  data={items}
  keyExtractor={(item) => item.id}
  renderItem={renderItem}
  extraData={selectedId}
  getItemLayout={(_, index) => ({
    length: ROW_HEIGHT,
    offset: ROW_HEIGHT * index,
    index,
  })}
/> 
```

React Native uses React to describe an application UI, but its core components map to native platform views rather than browser DOM elements. The JavaScript side contains components and application logic; native projects bundle that JavaScript, render native components, and expose platform capabilities. Share behavior and presentation where the platforms genuinely align, then isolate differences with platform APIs, platform-specific files, or small adapter components. Do not assume that a web CSS or browser API exists in React Native. Validate keyboard, permissions, navigation, accessibility, and system back behavior on both platforms. The goal is not identical pixels everywhere; it is a consistent product contract implemented in ways that respect each platform.

```tsx caption="Keep a small platform seam instead of scattering platform checks through business logic."
import { Platform, Text } from 'react-native';

export function PlatformLabel() {
  const label = Platform.select({
    ios: 'iOS',
    android: 'Android',
    default: 'Other',
  });

  return <Text accessibilityRole="text">Running on {label}</Text>;
}
```
